{"type":"user","message":{"role":"user","content":[{"tool_use_id":"call_14fbefec6f4c427bbf12da10","type":"tool_result","content":[{"type":"text","text":"I now have a thorough understanding of the entire backend. Here is my detailed performance optimization report.\n\n---\n\n## Performance Optimization Report: Python/FastAPI Backend\n\n---\n\n### 1. DUPLICATE DATABASE ENGINE INSTANCES (HIGH IMPACT)\n\n**Problem:** Every single router module creates its own `_get_engine()` singleton that independently calls `create_engine()` with the same database URL. This means there are **8 separate SQLAlchemy engine instances** for the same SQLite database, each with their own connection pool.\n\n**Files and lines:**\n- `/Users/katisarrow/essay_read/backend/routers/papers.py` lines 34-41\n- `/Users/katisarrow/essay_read/backend/routers/analysis.py` lines 26-33\n- `/Users/katisarrow/essay_read/backend/routers/code.py` lines 20-28\n- `/Users/katisarrow/essay_read/backend/routers/workspaces.py` lines 23-29\n- `/Users/katisarrow/essay_read/backend/routers/research.py` lines 31-38\n- `/Users/katisarrow/essay_read/backend/routers/tags.py` lines 15-22\n- `/Users/katisarrow/essay_read/backend/routers/notes.py` lines 18-24\n- `/Users/katisarrow/essay_read/backend/routers/annotations.py` lines 18-24\n- `/Users/katisarrow/essay_read/backend/services/database.py` line 139 (a separate one)\n- `/Users/katisarrow/essay_read/backend/services/analysis_runner.py` lines 35-42 (yet another)\n- `/Users/katisarrow/essay_read/backend/services/auto_indexer.py` lines 55-61 (another)\n\n**Recommendation:** Create a single shared engine in `database.py` and import it everywhere. For SQLite with `check_same_thread=False`, one engine is sufficient and correct.\n\n---\n\n### 2. N+1 QUERY PATTERNS IN WORKSPACE AND TAG LISTINGS (HIGH IMPACT)\n\n**Workspace listing -- N+1 queries:**\n`/Users/katisarrow/essay_read/backend/routers/workspaces.py` lines 63-73\n\nThe `list_workspaces` endpoint fetches all workspaces, then for each workspace runs a separate query to count its papers:\n```python\nfor ws in workspaces:\n    count_statement = select(WorkspacePaper).where(WorkspacePaper.workspace_id == ws.id)\n    paper_count = len(session.exec(count_statement).all())\n```\nThis is a classic N+1 pattern. For W workspaces, this is 1 + W queries.\n\n**Fix:** Use a single query with a `LEFT JOIN` and `GROUP BY` (or a subquery) to compute paper counts in one shot. SQLite supports this via `SELECT ws.*, COUNT(wp.id) FROM workspaces ws LEFT JOIN workspace_papers wp ON ws.id = wp.workspace_id GROUP BY ws.id`.\n\nThe same pattern appears in `update_workspace` at line 154-155.\n\n**Tag listing -- N+1 queries:**\n`/Users/katisarrow/essay_read/backend/routers/tags.py` lines 43-53\n\n```python\nfor tag in tags:\n    count = len(session.exec(\n        select(PaperTag).where(PaperTag.tag_id == tag.id)\n    ).all())\n```\nSame N+1 problem. Fix with a single query grouping PaperTag by tag_id.\n\n**Tags per paper -- N+1 queries:**\n`/Users/katisarrow/essay_read/backend/routers/tags.py` lines 108-115 (`get_paper_tags`) and lines 153-171 (`get_papers_by_tag`). Both iterate PaperTag records and then fetch each related object one at a time with `session.get()`.\n\n---\n\n### 3. SMART-SEARCH LOADS ALL PAPERS INTO MEMORY (HIGH IMPACT)\n\n`/Users/katisarrow/essay_read/backend/routers/papers.py` lines 183-270\n\nThe `smart_search` endpoint fetches **all matching papers** into memory with `session.exec(stmt).all()`, then for each paper queries its `AnalysisResult` individually (N+1), performs in-memory string matching, and finally sorts in Python. For large libraries this will be very slow.\n\n**Specific problems:**\n- Line 214: `papers = session.exec(stmt).all()` loads all papers without pagination at the DB level.\n- Line 235: `ar = session.exec(select(AR).where(AR.paper_id == p.id)).first()` is another N+1 query.\n- Lines 219-253: All search logic runs in Python rather than SQL. The `full_text` column search (`ql in p.full_text.lower()` at line 242) operates on the full text of every paper.\n\n**Recommendations:**\n- Use SQLite `LIKE` or FTS5 for text search instead of loading everything into memory.\n- Push pagination to the SQL level.\n- Join Paper with AnalysisResult in a single query instead of N+1.\n\n---\n\n### 4. SYNCHRONOUS DATABASE CALLS BLOCKING THE EVENT LOOP (MEDIUM IMPACT)\n\nMany router endpoints are declared `async` but perform synchronous SQLAlchemy operations directly (without `await asyncio.to_thread()`). Since SQLAlchemy with SQLite is synchronous, these calls block the uvicorn event loop.\n\n**Examples:**\n- `/Users/katisarrow/essay_read/backend/routers/papers.py` lines 80-83: `upload_paper` does `create_paper` and `get_paper` synchronously inside an `async` endpoint.\n- `/Users/katisarrow/essay_read/backend/routers/papers.py` lines 123-128: `get_papers_list` blocks.\n- `/Users/katisarrow/essay_read/backend/routers/analysis.py` lines 306-412: `_test_anthropic_connection` and `_test_embedding_connection` both block on synchronous `load_config()` calls that read from disk.\n- `/Users/katisarrow/essay_read/backend/routers/tags.py`: Every endpoint blocks.\n- `/Users/katisarrow/essay_read/backend/routers/notes.py`: Every endpoint blocks.\n- `/Users/katisarrow/essay_read/backend/routers/annotations.py`: Every endpoint blocks.\n\n**Impact:** While a single request may be fast, concurrent requests will serialize on these blocking calls. Under load this degrades throughput significantly.\n\n**Recommendation:** Either wrap synchronous DB calls in `asyncio.to_thread()` or use `run_in_executor`, or migrate to `aiosqlite` for truly async SQLite access.\n\n---\n\n### 5. PDF PARSING RUNS ON THE EVENT LOOP VIA asyncio.to_thread -- CORRECT, BUT REDUNDANT FULL RE-EXTRACTION (MEDIUM IMPACT)\n\n`/Users/katisarrow/essay_read/backend/services/pdf_parser.py` lines 169-226\n\nThe `extract_pdf_content` function correctly uses synchronous file I/O. It is called from `analysis_runner.py` line 232 via `asyncio.to_thread(extract_pdf_content, pdf_path)`.\n\nHowever, `auto_indexer.py` line 67-73 also extracts the full text of the PDF at upload time via `_extract(pdf_path)`. Then when the user triggers analysis, `analysis_runner.py` extracts it again from the same PDF. This is redundant work -- the `full_text` is already stored in the Paper model.\n\n**Recommendation:** In `_run_analysis`, check if `paper.full_text` is already populated (from auto_index) and reuse it instead of re-extracting the PDF. Only fall back to extraction if full_text is missing.\n\n---\n\n### 6. CONFIG FILE READ FROM DISK ON EVERY REQUEST (MEDIUM IMPACT)\n\n`/Users/katisarrow/essay_read/backend/services/ai_config.py` lines 121-135\n\n`load_config()` reads and parses the JSON config file from disk on every single call. It is called from multiple places per request:\n- `get_analysis_config()` reads from disk\n- `get_translate_config()` reads from disk\n- `get_embedding_config()` reads from disk\n- etc.\n\nIn the `text_action` endpoint (`/Users/katisarrow/essay_read/backend/routers/analysis.py` lines 427-472), `get_translate_config()` and `get_analysis_config()` are each called, each reading the file from disk independently.\n\n**Recommendation:** Add an in-memory cache with a short TTL (e.g., 5 seconds) or a file modification time check. Alternatively, use `watchdog` or similar to cache and invalidate on write.\n\n---\n\n### 7. `_match_content_fields` DESERIALIZES JSON FOR EVERY SEARCH HIT (MEDIUM IMPACT)\n\n`/Users/katisarrow/essay_read/backend/routers/papers.py` lines 160-180\n\n```python\nfor field, obj in ((\"method\", ar.method), (\"experiments\", ar.experiments)):\n    s = _json.dumps(obj or {}, ensure_ascii=False)\n    sl = s.lower()\n    if ql in sl:\n```\n\nThis serializes the `method` and `experiments` JSON fields to string and then does a case-insensitive substring search. If there are many search results, this is repeated for each one. Additionally, if the objects are already deserialized from SQLite JSON columns, re-serializing them is wasteful.\n\n---\n\n### 8. MISSING DATABASE INDEXES (MEDIUM IMPACT)\n\n`/Users/katisarrow/essay_read/backend/models/paper.py` and `/Users/katisarrow/essay_read/backend/models/analysis.py`\n\nThe Paper model has no explicit indexes. Queries that filter on `source`, `status`, `index_status`, `created_at`, `year`, and `category` (used extensively in `list_papers`, `smart_search`, `reindex_missing`, etc.) will do full table scans.\n\n**Missing indexes:**\n- `papers.source` -- filtered in `list_papers` (database.py line 388), `smart_search` (papers.py line 206), `_pick_reindex_targets` (auto_indexer.py line 249)\n- `papers.index_status` -- filtered in `reindex_missing` (auto_indexer.py line 222)\n- `papers.status` -- filtered in research.py line 62\n- `papers.year` -- filtered in `list_papers` (database.py line 399)\n- `papers.category` -- filtered in `list_papers` (database.py line 398)\n- `papers.created_at` -- used for ORDER BY in `list_papers` (database.py line 406)\n- `analysis_results.paper_id` -- has a unique constraint but queries always filter by this, should verify it creates an index\n\nThe `WorkspacePaper` model in `/Users/katisarrow/essay_read/backend/models/workspace.py` does have `index=True` on both FK columns (lines 33 and 37), which is good.\n\nThe `Tag` and `PaperTag` models likely lack indexes on commonly queried columns.\n\n---\n\n### 9. MEMORY LEAK: AnalysisJob EVENTS LIST GROWS UNBOUNDED (MEDIUM IMPACT)\n\n`/Users/katisarrow/essay_read/backend/services/analysis_runner.py` lines 110-113\n\n```python\nclass AnalysisJob:\n    def __init__(self, paper_id: int):\n        self.paper_id = paper_id\n        self.events: list[StreamEvent] = []          # 回放缓冲\n        self.subscribers: set[asyncio.Queue] = set()  # 实时订阅队列\n```\n\nThe `_jobs` dict (line 163) stores completed jobs indefinitely. Each completed job retains its full `events` list (which includes all AI output fields -- potentially large JSON blobs) and its `subscribers` set. Over time with many analyses, this will consume significant memory.\n\n**Recommendation:** Add cleanup logic to evict completed jobs from `_jobs` after a TTL (e.g., 30 minutes) or after all subscribers have disconnected.\n\n---\n\n### 10. Anthropic CLIENT CREATED AND CLOSED PER REQUEST (MEDIUM IMPACT)\n\n`/Users/katisarrow/essay_read/backend/services/ai_analyzer.py` lines 247-266, 311-312\n\nIn `analyze_paper_stream`, a new `AsyncAnthropic` client is created on every retry attempt:\n```python\nfor attempt in range(MAX_RETRIES + 1):\n    client = _create_client(api_key, base_url)\n```\n\nAnd then in `analysis_runner.py`, `code_analyzer.py`, `auto_tagger.py`, and `semantic_scholar.py`, every AI call creates and closes a new client. Creating an HTTP client involves TLS handshake setup, connection pool initialization, etc.\n\n**Specific locations creating new clients:**\n- `/Users/katisarrow/essay_read/backend/services/ai_analyzer.py` line 312\n- `/Users/katisarrow/essay_read/backend/services/code_analyzer.py` lines 618-619, 670-671, 718-719\n- `/Users/katisarrow/essay_read/backend/services/auto_tagger.py` line 48\n- `/Users/katisarrow/essay_read/backend/services/semantic_scholar.py` line 207\n- `/Users/katisarrow/essay_read/backend/routers/analysis.py` lines 342, 457-458\n\n**Recommendation:** Reuse a single `AsyncAnthropic` client instance for each API key/base_url combination. The Anthropic SDK supports this pattern.\n\n---\n\n### 11. REDUNDANT DATABASE QUERIES IN `get_paper_pdf` (LOW-MEDIUM IMPACT)\n\n`/Users/katisarrow/essay_read/backend/routers/papers.py` lines 430-465\n\nThe `get_paper_pdf` endpoint queries the database **twice** for the same paper:\n1. Line 434-439: `get_paper(session, paper_id)` to check existence\n2. Line 444-453: `session.get(PaperModel, paper_id)` to get `pdf_path`\n\nThe first call already returns the `pdf_path` (see `get_paper` in database.py line 282-300), so the second query is entirely redundant.\n\nSimilarly, `export_paper` (lines 387-424) opens two separate sessions to fetch paper and analysis, when both could be done in one session.\n\n---\n\n### 12. ANALYSIS STREAMING ENDPOINT OPENS MULTIPLE SESSIONS (LOW IMPACT)\n\n`/Users/katisarrow/essay_read/backend/routers/analysis.py` lines 769-882\n\nThe `stream_analysis` endpoint opens a Session to get paper status, then opens additional Sessions inside `event_generator()` for the same data. For the \"done\" case (line 796-798), it opens yet another session to fetch the analysis.\n\n---\n\n### 13. `_truncate_text` BUILDS FULL TEXT TWICE FOR LONG PAPERS (LOW IMPACT)\n\n`/Users/katisarrow/essay_read/backend/services/pdf_parser.py` lines 63-101\n\nWhen a paper exceeds `MAX_CHARS`, the function first builds the complete text (`full = \"\\n\".join(...)`) to check the length (line 76-80), and then if it exceeds the limit, builds the truncated version by iterating through pages again. For a 100+ page PDF, this means processing all pages twice.\n\n**Recommendation:** Calculate total character count incrementally during the first pass and only build the full string if it fits.\n\n---\n\n### 14. `asyncio.create_task` WITHOUT ERROR HANDLING (LOW-MEDIUM IMPACT)\n\n`/Users/katisarrow/essay_read/backend/main.py` line 61:\n```python\nasyncio.create_task(reindex_missing())\n```\n\n`/Users/katisarrow/essay_read/backend/routers/papers.py` line 87:\n```python\nasyncio.create_task(auto_index(paper_id, pdf_path))\n```\n\n`/Users/katisarrow/essay_read/backend/routers/research.py` line 83:\n```python\nasyncio.create_task(run_pipeline(run_id))\n```\n\nWhen `asyncio.create_task` is used without storing the task reference or adding an exception callback, if the task raises an exception, it is silently lost (only logged by the event loop's default handler). This is a reliability and debugging issue, not directly a performance issue, but unhandled background task failures can lead to inconsistent state.\n\n---\n\n### 15. FINDPAPERS SYNC CALLS ON THREAD POOL (LOW IMPACT)\n\n`/Users/katisarrow/essay_read/backend/services/findpapers_service.py` lines 46-49, 52-119\n\n`_get_engine()` creates a new `findpapers.Engine()` on every call. The `findpapers` library itself may do blocking network I/O internally. Since this is wrapped in `asyncio.to_thread()`, the blocking is contained, but the engine is recreated each time. If the engine maintains any state or connection pool, this is wasteful.\n\n---\n\n### 16. NO RESPONSE CACHING ON READ-HEAVY ENDPOINTS (LOW-MEDIUM IMPACT)\n\nSeveral endpoints return data that changes infrequently but are queried repeatedly:\n- `GET /api/analysis/config` (analysis.py line 141) -- config rarely changes\n- `GET /api/papers/{paper_id}` -- paper metadata is stable after indexing\n- `GET /api/analysis/{paper_id}` -- analysis results are immutable once done\n- `GET /api/code/{paper_id}/analysis` -- code analysis results are immutable once done\n- `GET /api/tags` -- tag list changes rarely\n\nNone of these endpoints use HTTP caching headers (`ETag`, `Last-Modified`, `Cache-Control`). The SSE endpoints correctly set `no-cache`, but the regular GET endpoints could benefit from caching.\n\n---\n\n### 17. `full_text` COLUMN STORES 300K CHARACTERS PER PAPER (LOW-MEDIUM IMPACT)\n\n`/Users/katisarrow/essay_read/backend/models/paper.py` line 39 and `/Users/katisarrow/essay_read/backend/services/auto_indexer.py` line 26\n\n```python\nfull_text: Optional[str] = None  # PyMuPDF 提取的全文（截断 ~300k）\nFULL_TEXT_LIMIT = 300_000\n```\n\nStoring 300KB of text per paper directly in the SQLite `papers` table has several consequences:\n- Every `SELECT *` or column-based query on the papers table must handle this large column.\n- The `smart_search` endpoint (papers.py line 242) does `ql in p.full_text.lower()` which materializes the full 300K string and does a linear scan.\n- Database file size grows quickly.\n\n**Recommendation:** Move `full_text` to a separate table so it is only loaded when needed (e.g., for search or indexing), not on every paper list/detail query.\n\n---\n\n### 18. TERMINAL WEBSOCKET USES POLLING INSTEAD OF SELECT-BASED I/O (LOW IMPACT)\n\n`/Users/katisarrow/essay_read/backend/services/terminal.py` lines 131-141\n\nThe `read_from_pty` function uses `loop.run_in_executor(None, session.read)` which calls `select.select()` with a 50ms timeout in a tight loop, then sleeps 10ms if no data. This busy-polling pattern wastes CPU. A better approach would be to use `asyncio.loop.add_reader()` on the master_fd to be notified when data is available, avoiding the polling loop entirely.\n\n---\n\n### Summary of Priorities\n\n| Priority | Issue | Impact |\n|----------|-------|--------|\n| HIGH | Duplicate engine instances (8+ separate engines) | Connection pool waste, memory |\n| HIGH | N+1 queries in workspace/tag listings | Slow page loads |\n| HIGH | Smart-search loads all papers into memory | OOM risk, very slow |\n| MEDIUM | Sync DB calls blocking event loop | Reduced concurrency |\n| MEDIUM | Redundant PDF extraction | Wasted CPU and I/O |\n| MEDIUM | Config file read from disk on every call | Disk I/O on every request |\n| MEDIUM | Missing database indexes | Slow queries as data grows |\n| MEDIUM | AnalysisJob events list memory leak | Memory growth over time |\n| MEDIUM | New HTTP client per AI call | TLS overhead per request |\n| MEDIUM | No HTTP caching headers | Redundant data transfer |\n| LOW-MED | full_text stored inline in papers table | DB bloat, slow queries |\n| LOW | Redundant DB queries in get_paper_pdf | Unnecessary I/O |\n| LOW | Truncation builds text twice | Minor CPU waste |\n| LOW | Terminal busy-polling | Minor CPU waste |"}]}]},"parent_tool_use_id":null,"session_id":"29257b8f-ede7-46a4-8e0f-a743591d296a","uuid":"656149ee-4068-4933-bd43-5f8959e8fe55","timestamp":"2026-06-09T05:11:24.351Z","tool_use_result":{"status":"completed","prompt":"I need to identify performance optimization opportunities in the Python/FastAPI backend. Focus on:\n\n1. Read backend/main.py to understand the app structure and middleware\n2. Check backend/services/database.py for any N+1 query issues or missing indexes\n3. Look at backend/routers/ for any endpoints that could benefit from caching\n4. Check backend/services/ai_analyzer.py for any inefficient API calls\n5. Look at backend/services/pdf_parser.py for any performance bottlenecks\n6. Check if there are any synchronous operations that could be async\n7. Look for any memory leaks or resource management issues\n8. Check if static files are served efficiently\n\nProvide a detailed report of findings with specific file paths and line numbers.","agentId":"aee0fe252a50231ea","agentType":"Explore","content":[{"type":"text","text":"I now have a thorough understanding of the entire backend. Here is my detailed performance optimization report.\n\n---\n\n## Performance Optimization Report: Python/FastAPI Backend\n\n---\n\n### 1. DUPLICATE DATABASE ENGINE INSTANCES (HIGH IMPACT)\n\n**Problem:** Every single router module creates its own `_get_engine()` singleton that independently calls `create_engine()` with the same database URL. This means there are **8 separate SQLAlchemy engine instances** for the same SQLite database, each with their own connection pool.\n\n**Files and lines:**\n- `/Users/katisarrow/essay_read/backend/routers/papers.py` lines 34-41\n- `/Users/katisarrow/essay_read/backend/routers/analysis.py` lines 26-33\n- `/Users/katisarrow/essay_read/backend/routers/code.py` lines 20-28\n- `/Users/katisarrow/essay_read/backend/routers/workspaces.py` lines 23-29\n- `/Users/katisarrow/essay_read/backend/routers/research.py` lines 31-38\n- `/Users/katisarrow/essay_read/backend/routers/tags.py` lines 15-22\n- `/Users/katisarrow/essay_read/backend/routers/notes.py` lines 18-24\n- `/Users/katisarrow/essay_read/backend/routers/annotations.py` lines 18-24\n- `/Users/katisarrow/essay_read/backend/services/database.py` line 139 (a separate one)\n- `/Users/katisarrow/essay_read/backend/services/analysis_runner.py` lines 35-42 (yet another)\n- `/Users/katisarrow/essay_read/backend/services/auto_indexer.py` lines 55-61 (another)\n\n**Recommendation:** Create a single shared engine in `database.py` and import it everywhere. For SQLite with `check_same_thread=False`, one engine is sufficient and correct.\n\n---\n\n### 2. N+1 QUERY PATTERNS IN WORKSPACE AND TAG LISTINGS (HIGH IMPACT)\n\n**Workspace listing -- N+1 queries:**\n`/Users/katisarrow/essay_read/backend/routers/workspaces.py` lines 63-73\n\nThe `list_workspaces` endpoint fetches all workspaces, then for each workspace runs a separate query to count its papers:\n```python\nfor ws in workspaces:\n    count_statement = select(WorkspacePaper).where(WorkspacePaper.workspace_id == ws.id)\n    paper_count = len(session.exec(count_statement).all())\n```\nThis is a classic N+1 pattern. For W workspaces, this is 1 + W queries.\n\n**Fix:** Use a single query with a `LEFT JOIN` and `GROUP BY` (or a subquery) to compute paper counts in one shot. SQLite supports this via `SELECT ws.*, COUNT(wp.id) FROM workspaces ws LEFT JOIN workspace_papers wp ON ws.id = wp.workspace_id GROUP BY ws.id`.\n\nThe same pattern appears in `update_workspace` at line 154-155.\n\n**Tag listing -- N+1 queries:**\n`/Users/katisarrow/essay_read/backend/routers/tags.py` lines 43-53\n\n```python\nfor tag in tags:\n    count = len(session.exec(\n        select(PaperTag).where(PaperTag.tag_id == tag.id)\n    ).all())\n```\nSame N+1 problem. Fix with a single query grouping PaperTag by tag_id.\n\n**Tags per paper -- N+1 queries:**\n`/Users/katisarrow/essay_read/backend/routers/tags.py` lines 108-115 (`get_paper_tags`) and lines 153-171 (`get_papers_by_tag`). Both iterate PaperTag records and then fetch each related object one at a time with `session.get()`.\n\n---\n\n### 3. SMART-SEARCH LOADS ALL PAPERS INTO MEMORY (HIGH IMPACT)\n\n`/Users/katisarrow/essay_read/backend/routers/papers.py` lines 183-270\n\nThe `smart_search` endpoint fetches **all matching papers** into memory with `session.exec(stmt).all()`, then for each paper queries its `AnalysisResult` individually (N+1), performs in-memory string matching, and finally sorts in Python. For large libraries this will be very slow.\n\n**Specific problems:**\n- Line 214: `papers = session.exec(stmt).all()` loads all papers without pagination at the DB level.\n- Line 235: `ar = session.exec(select(AR).where(AR.paper_id == p.id)).first()` is another N+1 query.\n- Lines 219-253: All search logic runs in Python rather than SQL. The `full_text` column search (`ql in p.full_text.lower()` at line 242) operates on the full text of every paper.\n\n**Recommendations:**\n- Use SQLite `LIKE` or FTS5 for text search instead of loading everything into memory.\n- Push pagination to the SQL level.\n- Join Paper with AnalysisResult in a single query instead of N+1.\n\n---\n\n### 4. SYNCHRONOUS DATABASE CALLS BLOCKING THE EVENT LOOP (MEDIUM IMPACT)\n\nMany router endpoints are declared `async` but perform synchronous SQLAlchemy operations directly (without `await asyncio.to_thread()`). Since SQLAlchemy with SQLite is synchronous, these calls block the uvicorn event loop.\n\n**Examples:**\n- `/Users/katisarrow/essay_read/backend/routers/papers.py` lines 80-83: `upload_paper` does `create_paper` and `get_paper` synchronously inside an `async` endpoint.\n- `/Users/katisarrow/essay_read/backend/routers/papers.py` lines 123-128: `get_papers_list` blocks.\n- `/Users/katisarrow/essay_read/backend/routers/analysis.py` lines 306-412: `_test_anthropic_connection` and `_test_embedding_connection` both block on synchronous `load_config()` calls that read from disk.\n- `/Users/katisarrow/essay_read/backend/routers/tags.py`: Every endpoint blocks.\n- `/Users/katisarrow/essay_read/backend/routers/notes.py`: Every endpoint blocks.\n- `/Users/katisarrow/essay_read/backend/routers/annotations.py`: Every endpoint blocks.\n\n**Impact:** While a single request may be fast, concurrent requests will serialize on these blocking calls. Under load this degrades throughput significantly.\n\n**Recommendation:** Either wrap synchronous DB calls in `asyncio.to_thread()` or use `run_in_executor`, or migrate to `aiosqlite` for truly async SQLite access.\n\n---\n\n### 5. PDF PARSING RUNS ON THE EVENT LOOP VIA asyncio.to_thread -- CORRECT, BUT REDUNDANT FULL RE-EXTRACTION (MEDIUM IMPACT)\n\n`/Users/katisarrow/essay_read/backend/services/pdf_parser.py` lines 169-226\n\nThe `extract_pdf_content` function correctly uses synchronous file I/O. It is called from `analysis_runner.py` line 232 via `asyncio.to_thread(extract_pdf_content, pdf_path)`.\n\nHowever, `auto_indexer.py` line 67-73 also extracts the full text of the PDF at upload time via `_extract(pdf_path)`. Then when the user triggers analysis, `analysis_runner.py` extracts it again from the same PDF. This is redundant work -- the `full_text` is already stored in the Paper model.\n\n**Recommendation:** In `_run_analysis`, check if `paper.full_text` is already populated (from auto_index) and reuse it instead of re-extracting the PDF. Only fall back to extraction if full_text is missing.\n\n---\n\n### 6. CONFIG FILE READ FROM DISK ON EVERY REQUEST (MEDIUM IMPACT)\n\n`/Users/katisarrow/essay_read/backend/services/ai_config.py` lines 121-135\n\n`load_config()` reads and parses the JSON config file from disk on every single call. It is called from multiple places per request:\n- `get_analysis_config()` reads from disk\n- `get_translate_config()` reads from disk\n- `get_embedding_config()` reads from disk\n- etc.\n\nIn the `text_action` endpoint (`/Users/katisarrow/essay_read/backend/routers/analysis.py` lines 427-472), `get_translate_config()` and `get_analysis_config()` are each called, each reading the file from disk independently.\n\n**Recommendation:** Add an in-memory cache with a short TTL (e.g., 5 seconds) or a file modification time check. Alternatively, use `watchdog` or similar to cache and invalidate on write.\n\n---\n\n### 7. `_match_content_fields` DESERIALIZES JSON FOR EVERY SEARCH HIT (MEDIUM IMPACT)\n\n`/Users/katisarrow/essay_read/backend/routers/papers.py` lines 160-180\n\n```python\nfor field, obj in ((\"method\", ar.method), (\"experiments\", ar.experiments)):\n    s = _json.dumps(obj or {}, ensure_ascii=False)\n    sl = s.lower()\n    if ql in sl:\n```\n\nThis serializes the `method` and `experiments` JSON fields to string and then does a case-insensitive substring search. If there are many search results, this is repeated for each one. Additionally, if the objects are already deserialized from SQLite JSON columns, re-serializing them is wasteful.\n\n---\n\n### 8. MISSING DATABASE INDEXES (MEDIUM IMPACT)\n\n`/Users/katisarrow/essay_read/backend/models/paper.py` and `/Users/katisarrow/essay_read/backend/models/analysis.py`\n\nThe Paper model has no explicit indexes. Queries that filter on `source`, `status`, `index_status`, `created_at`, `year`, and `category` (used extensively in `list_papers`, `smart_search`, `reindex_missing`, etc.) will do full table scans.\n\n**Missing indexes:**\n- `papers.source` -- filtered in `list_papers` (database.py line 388), `smart_search` (papers.py line 206), `_pick_reindex_targets` (auto_indexer.py line 249)\n- `papers.index_status` -- filtered in `reindex_missing` (auto_indexer.py line 222)\n- `papers.status` -- filtered in research.py line 62\n- `papers.year` -- filtered in `list_papers` (database.py line 399)\n- `papers.category` -- filtered in `list_papers` (database.py line 398)\n- `papers.created_at` -- used for ORDER BY in `list_papers` (database.py line 406)\n- `analysis_results.paper_id` -- has a unique constraint but queries always filter by this, should verify it creates an index\n\nThe `WorkspacePaper` model in `/Users/katisarrow/essay_read/backend/models/workspace.py` does have `index=True` on both FK columns (lines 33 and 37), which is good.\n\nThe `Tag` and `PaperTag` models likely lack indexes on commonly queried columns.\n\n---\n\n### 9. MEMORY LEAK: AnalysisJob EVENTS LIST GROWS UNBOUNDED (MEDIUM IMPACT)\n\n`/Users/katisarrow/essay_read/backend/services/analysis_runner.py` lines 110-113\n\n```python\nclass AnalysisJob:\n    def __init__(self, paper_id: int):\n        self.paper_id = paper_id\n        self.events: list[StreamEvent] = []          # 回放缓冲\n        self.subscribers: set[asyncio.Queue] = set()  # 实时订阅队列\n```\n\nThe `_jobs` dict (line 163) stores completed jobs indefinitely. Each completed job retains its full `events` list (which includes all AI output fields -- potentially large JSON blobs) and its `subscribers` set. Over time with many analyses, this will consume significant memory.\n\n**Recommendation:** Add cleanup logic to evict completed jobs from `_jobs` after a TTL (e.g., 30 minutes) or after all subscribers have disconnected.\n\n---\n\n### 10. Anthropic CLIENT CREATED AND CLOSED PER REQUEST (MEDIUM IMPACT)\n\n`/Users/katisarrow/essay_read/backend/services/ai_analyzer.py` lines 247-266, 311-312\n\nIn `analyze_paper_stream`, a new `AsyncAnthropic` client is created on every retry attempt:\n```python\nfor attempt in range(MAX_RETRIES + 1):\n    client = _create_client(api_key, base_url)\n```\n\nAnd then in `analysis_runner.py`, `code_analyzer.py`, `auto_tagger.py`, and `semantic_scholar.py`, every AI call creates and closes a new client. Creating an HTTP client involves TLS handshake setup, connection pool initialization, etc.\n\n**Specific locations creating new clients:**\n- `/Users/katisarrow/essay_read/backend/services/ai_analyzer.py` line 312\n- `/Users/katisarrow/essay_read/backend/services/code_analyzer.py` lines 618-619, 670-671, 718-719\n- `/Users/katisarrow/essay_read/backend/services/auto_tagger.py` line 48\n- `/Users/katisarrow/essay_read/backend/services/semantic_scholar.py` line 207\n- `/Users/katisarrow/essay_read/backend/routers/analysis.py` lines 342, 457-458\n\n**Recommendation:** Reuse a single `AsyncAnthropic` client instance for each API key/base_url combination. The Anthropic SDK supports this pattern.\n\n---\n\n### 11. REDUNDANT DATABASE QUERIES IN `get_paper_pdf` (LOW-MEDIUM IMPACT)\n\n`/Users/katisarrow/essay_read/backend/routers/papers.py` lines 430-465\n\nThe `get_paper_pdf` endpoint queries the database **twice** for the same paper:\n1. Line 434-439: `get_paper(session, paper_id)` to check existence\n2. Line 444-453: `session.get(PaperModel, paper_id)` to get `pdf_path`\n\nThe first call already returns the `pdf_path` (see `get_paper` in database.py line 282-300), so the second query is entirely redundant.\n\nSimilarly, `export_paper` (lines 387-424) opens two separate sessions to fetch paper and analysis, when both could be done in one session.\n\n---\n\n### 12. ANALYSIS STREAMING ENDPOINT OPENS MULTIPLE SESSIONS (LOW IMPACT)\n\n`/Users/katisarrow/essay_read/backend/routers/analysis.py` lines 769-882\n\nThe `stream_analysis` endpoint opens a Session to get paper status, then opens additional Sessions inside `event_generator()` for the same data. For the \"done\" case (line 796-798), it opens yet another session to fetch the analysis.\n\n---\n\n### 13. `_truncate_text` BUILDS FULL TEXT TWICE FOR LONG PAPERS (LOW IMPACT)\n\n`/Users/katisarrow/essay_read/backend/services/pdf_parser.py` lines 63-101\n\nWhen a paper exceeds `MAX_CHARS`, the function first builds the complete text (`full = \"\\n\".join(...)`) to check the length (line 76-80), and then if it exceeds the limit, builds the truncated version by iterating through pages again. For a 100+ page PDF, this means processing all pages twice.\n\n**Recommendation:** Calculate total character count incrementally during the first pass and only build the full string if it fits.\n\n---\n\n### 14. `asyncio.create_task` WITHOUT ERROR HANDLING (LOW-MEDIUM IMPACT)\n\n`/Users/katisarrow/essay_read/backend/main.py` line 61:\n```python\nasyncio.create_task(reindex_missing())\n```\n\n`/Users/katisarrow/essay_read/backend/routers/papers.py` line 87:\n```python\nasyncio.create_task(auto_index(paper_id, pdf_path))\n```\n\n`/Users/katisarrow/essay_read/backend/routers/research.py` line 83:\n```python\nasyncio.create_task(run_pipeline(run_id))\n```\n\nWhen `asyncio.create_task` is used without storing the task reference or adding an exception callback, if the task raises an exception, it is silently lost (only logged by the event loop's default handler). This is a reliability and debugging issue, not directly a performance issue, but unhandled background task failures can lead to inconsistent state.\n\n---\n\n### 15. FINDPAPERS SYNC CALLS ON THREAD POOL (LOW IMPACT)\n\n`/Users/katisarrow/essay_read/backend/services/findpapers_service.py` lines 46-49, 52-119\n\n`_get_engine()` creates a new `findpapers.Engine()` on every call. The `findpapers` library itself may do blocking network I/O internally. Since this is wrapped in `asyncio.to_thread()`, the blocking is contained, but the engine is recreated each time. If the engine maintains any state or connection pool, this is wasteful.\n\n---\n\n### 16. NO RESPONSE CACHING ON READ-HEAVY ENDPOINTS (LOW-MEDIUM IMPACT)\n\nSeveral endpoints return data that changes infrequently but are queried repeatedly:\n- `GET /api/analysis/config` (analysis.py line 141) -- config rarely changes\n- `GET /api/papers/{paper_id}` -- paper metadata is stable after indexing\n- `GET /api/analysis/{paper_id}` -- analysis results are immutable once done\n- `GET /api/code/{paper_id}/analysis` -- code analysis results are immutable once done\n- `GET /api/tags` -- tag list changes rarely\n\nNone of these endpoints use HTTP caching headers (`ETag`, `Last-Modified`, `Cache-Control`). The SSE endpoints correctly set `no-cache`, but the regular GET endpoints could benefit from caching.\n\n---\n\n### 17. `full_text` COLUMN STORES 300K CHARACTERS PER PAPER (LOW-MEDIUM IMPACT)\n\n`/Users/katisarrow/essay_read/backend/models/paper.py` line 39 and `/Users/katisarrow/essay_read/backend/services/auto_indexer.py` line 26\n\n```python\nfull_text: Optional[str] = None  # PyMuPDF 提取的全文（截断 ~300k）\nFULL_TEXT_LIMIT = 300_000\n```\n\nStoring 300KB of text per paper directly in the SQLite `papers` table has several consequences:\n- Every `SELECT *` or column-based query on the papers table must handle this large column.\n- The `smart_search` endpoint (papers.py line 242) does `ql in p.full_text.lower()` which materializes the full 300K string and does a linear scan.\n- Database file size grows quickly.\n\n**Recommendation:** Move `full_text` to a separate table so it is only loaded when needed (e.g., for search or indexing), not on every paper list/detail query.\n\n---\n\n### 18. TERMINAL WEBSOCKET USES POLLING INSTEAD OF SELECT-BASED I/O (LOW IMPACT)\n\n`/Users/katisarrow/essay_read/backend/services/terminal.py` lines 131-141\n\nThe `read_from_pty` function uses `loop.run_in_executor(None, session.read)` which calls `select.select()` with a 50ms timeout in a tight loop, then sleeps 10ms if no data. This busy-polling pattern wastes CPU. A better approach would be to use `asyncio.loop.add_reader()` on the master_fd to be notified when data is available, avoiding the polling loop entirely.\n\n---\n\n### Summary of Priorities\n\n| Priority | Issue | Impact |\n|----------|-------|--------|\n| HIGH | Duplicate engine instances (8+ separate engines) | Connection pool waste, memory |\n| HIGH | N+1 queries in workspace/tag listings | Slow page loads |\n| HIGH | Smart-search loads all papers into memory | OOM risk, very slow |\n| MEDIUM | Sync DB calls blocking event loop | Reduced concurrency |\n| MEDIUM | Redundant PDF extraction | Wasted CPU and I/O |\n| MEDIUM | Config file read from disk on every call | Disk I/O on every request |\n| MEDIUM | Missing database indexes | Slow queries as data grows |\n| MEDIUM | AnalysisJob events list memory leak | Memory growth over time |\n| MEDIUM | New HTTP client per AI call | TLS overhead per request |\n| MEDIUM | No HTTP caching headers | Redundant data transfer |\n| LOW-MED | full_text stored inline in papers table | DB bloat, slow queries |\n| LOW | Redundant DB queries in get_paper_pdf | Unnecessary I/O |\n| LOW | Truncation builds text twice | Minor CPU waste |\n| LOW | Terminal busy-polling | Minor CPU waste |"}],"totalDurationMs":73836,"totalTokens":108371,"totalToolUseCount":33,"usage":{"input_tokens":5251,"cache_creation_input_tokens":0,"cache_read_input_tokens":98624,"output_tokens":4496,"server_tool_use":{"web_search_requests":0,"web_fetch_requests":0},"service_tier":"standard","cache_creation":{"ephemeral_1h_input_tokens":0,"ephemeral_5m_input_tokens":0},"inference_geo":"","iterations":[],"speed":"standard"},"toolStats":{"readCount":28,"searchCount":1,"bashCount":4,"editFileCount":0,"linesAdded":0,"linesRemoved":0,"otherToolCount":0}}}
