{"type":"user","message":{"role":"user","content":[{"tool_use_id":"call_bddd82a75f2e46b98e4517cc","type":"tool_result","content":[{"type":"text","text":"I now have a thorough understanding of the entire codebase. Here is my detailed performance optimization report.\n\n---\n\n## Performance Optimization Report for essay_read React + TypeScript Frontend\n\n### 1. CODE SPLITTING (Already Implemented -- Well Done)\n\n**File:** `/Users/katisarrow/essay_read/frontend/src/App.tsx`, lines 26-34\n\nAll 9 page routes are already lazy-loaded with `React.lazy()`. The `TerminalPanel` component also lazy-loads the `RealTerminal` module at `/Users/katisarrow/essay_read/frontend/src/components/TerminalPanel.tsx`, line 4. This is a strong baseline -- initial bundle only needs to load the Layout shell and whichever page route the user hits.\n\n**One remaining opportunity:** The heavy feature components inside pages are NOT lazy-loaded. For example, `PdfViewer` (374 lines, imports `react-pdf` + `pdfjs-dist`), `GraphCanvas` (362 lines, imports `react-force-graph-2d`), `FlowChart` (imports `mermaid`), and `CodeAnalysisPanel` (531 lines, imports `mermaid`). These are eagerly bundled within their parent page chunks. Lazy-loading `GraphCanvas`, `FlowChart`, and `CodeAnalysisPanel` behind the tabs that actually render them would further shrink the initial parse cost of the `ReaderPage` and `CodePage` chunks.\n\n---\n\n### 2. ZERO USE OF React.memo\n\n**Finding:** There is not a single `React.memo()` call anywhere in the codebase. The grep for `React.memo` returned zero results.\n\nThis is the single most impactful optimization gap. The following components receive complex, frequently-changing props and would benefit from `React.memo`:\n\n- **`AnnotationLayer`** (`/Users/katisarrow/essay_read/frontend/src/features/viewer/AnnotationLayer.tsx`) -- receives the full `annotations` array (which gets new entries on every create) and a `pageNumber` number. It filters annotations on every render (line 13: `annotations.filter(a => a.page_number === pageNumber)`). With 20+ PDF pages rendered simultaneously in `PdfViewer`, each page re-renders whenever any annotation is added or deleted anywhere.\n\n- **`Sidebar`** (`/Users/katisarrow/essay_read/frontend/src/components/Sidebar.tsx`) -- receives `papers` array that gets fully replaced on fetch. It already has a `useMemo` for filtered papers (line 27), but the component itself re-renders on every parent state change.\n\n- **`PaperList`** (`/Users/katisarrow/essay_read/frontend/src/features/library/PaperList.tsx`) -- 402 lines, receives 17 props. Many are callbacks that change on every render of `LibraryPage` unless wrapped in `useCallback`.\n\n- **`OverviewTab`** (`/Users/katisarrow/essay_read/frontend/src/features/analysis/tabs/OverviewTab.tsx`) -- 579 lines, the largest tab component. It is re-rendered every time the `AnalysisPanel` parent re-renders (e.g., when switching tabs, when the activeTab state changes), even when its props have not changed.\n\n- **`OutlineTab`**, **`MethodTab`**, **`ExperimentsTab`**, **`InterviewTab`** -- all purely display components that receive `result` and should skip re-renders when `result` is referentially stable.\n\n- **`LogLine`** (`/Users/katisarrow/essay_read/frontend/src/components/TerminalPanel.tsx`, line 33) -- rendered in lists of up to 500 entries. Without `React.memo`, adding a new log entry causes ALL existing `LogLine` components to re-render.\n\n- **`ToastContainer`** (`/Users/katisarrow/essay_read/frontend/src/components/ToastContainer.tsx`) -- individual toast items are re-rendered as a batch when any toast is added or dismissed.\n\n---\n\n### 3. UNSTABLE PROP REFERENCES CAUSING UNNECESSARY RE-RENDERS\n\n**`ReaderPage`** (`/Users/katisarrow/essay_read/frontend/src/pages/ReaderPage.tsx`, lines 299-308):\n```tsx\n<PdfViewer\n  ref={pdfViewerRef}\n  pdfUrl={pdfUrl}\n  paperId={paperId}\n  activePage={activePage}\n  onPageChange={onPageChangeNoop}\n/>\n```\nThe `onPageChangeNoop` (line 37) is stable via `useCallback`, which is good. However, `activePage` changes frequently during scroll (via `IntersectionObserver`), and `PdfViewer` is not memoized, so every scroll-triggered page visibility update re-renders the entire PdfViewer.\n\n**`AnalysisPanel`** (`/Users/katisarrow/essay_read/frontend/src/features/analysis/AnalysisPanel.tsx`, lines 343-355):\n```tsx\n<AnalysisPanel\n  result={analysis}\n  partialResult={partialResult}\n  status={status}\n  progressMessage={progressMessage}\n  progress={progress}\n  relatedStatus={relatedStatus}\n  onJumpToPage={handleOutlineJump}\n  paperId={paperId}\n  onStartAnalysis={startAnalysis}\n  onReanalyze={reparse}\n/>\n```\nThe `partialResult` object is created fresh on every SSE `field` event via spread (in `useStreaming.ts` line 115: `setPartialResult(prev => ({ ...prev, [fieldName]: value }))`). This means `AnalysisPanel` re-renders on every field received during streaming analysis, and without memoization, it cascades to all 7 tab components. Since only `LoadingState` needs the partial result during streaming, this is wasteful when the user is on the `done` state viewing an analysis tab.\n\n**`ResearchPage`** (`/Users/katisarrow/essay_read/frontend/src/pages/ResearchPage.tsx`, lines 137-139):\n```tsx\nonSelectNode={(n) => { setSelectedNode(n); if (n) setSelectedEdge(null) }}\nonSelectEdge={(e) => { setSelectedEdge(e); if (e) setSelectedNode(null) }}\n```\nInline arrow functions are created on every render, defeating any future `React.memo` on `GraphCanvas`. These should be wrapped in `useCallback`.\n\n---\n\n### 4. HEAVY COMPUTATIONS IN RENDER PATHS\n\n**`PdfViewer` -- All pages rendered simultaneously** (`/Users/katisarrow/essay_read/frontend/src/features/viewer/PdfViewer.tsx`, lines 263-299):\n```tsx\n{Array.from({ length: numPages }, (_, i) => i + 1).map(pageNum => (\n  <Page pageNumber={pageNum} ... />\n))}\n```\nEvery single page of the PDF is rendered into the DOM simultaneously. For a 30-page paper, this means 30 `react-pdf` `<Page>` components plus 30 `<AnnotationLayer>` components all mount at once. This is extremely expensive in both memory and initial render time. A virtualized approach (rendering only visible pages plus a buffer) would dramatically reduce memory usage and first-paint time.\n\n**`FlowChart` -- Mermaid parsing on every render** (`/Users/katisarrow/essay_read/frontend/src/features/analysis/FlowChart.tsx`, lines 87-115):\nThe `useEffect` at line 87 re-runs `mermaid.render()` whenever the `chart` prop changes. While `mermaid.render()` is async, it is CPU-intensive and generates new DOM. The component does not use `useMemo` to cache the parsed SVG. If the parent re-renders (e.g., because a sibling tab was clicked in `AnalysisPanel`), the chart re-renders even if `chart` has not changed. Wrapping `FlowChart` in `React.memo` would prevent this.\n\n**`GraphCanvas` -- Force simulation polling** (`/Users/katisarrow/essay_read/frontend/src/features/research/GraphCanvas.tsx`, lines 226-233):\n```tsx\nuseEffect(() => {\n  const t = setInterval(() => {\n    const z = fgRef.current?.zoom?.()\n    if (typeof z === 'number' && Math.abs(z - zoomLevel) > 0.01) {\n      setZoomLevel(z)\n    }\n  }, 300)\n  return () => clearInterval(t)\n}, [zoomLevel])\n```\nThis polls every 300ms to sync the zoom level display, calling `setZoomLevel` which triggers a re-render of the entire `GraphCanvas` component (and all its children) even when the zoom percentage displayed in the UI has not meaningfully changed. This is a classic anti-pattern -- use a ref for the displayed value and only update state when the displayed text actually changes.\n\n**`ExperimentsTab` -- CSS variable reads in useMemo** (`/Users/katisarrow/essay_read/frontend/src/features/analysis/tabs/ExperimentsTab.tsx`, lines 23-38):\n`useThemeColors()` reads `getComputedStyle` on every theme change. The dependency array correctly depends on `ui.theme`, but `useUIConfig()` returns a new object reference on every config refresh (line 104 of `useConfig.ts`: `return ui` where `ui` is constructed with spread), which triggers the `useMemo` to recompute even when the theme has not changed.\n\n---\n\n### 5. HOOKS THAT CAUSE UNNECESSARY RE-RENDERS\n\n**`useUIConfig`** (`/Users/katisarrow/essay_read/frontend/src/hooks/useConfig.ts`, lines 85-105):\nEvery call to `useUIConfig()` creates a listener that calls `fetchConfig().then(cfg => setUI({...saved, ...previewOverrides}))`. The `setUI` call always creates a new object reference via spread, even when the config has not changed. This means every component using `useUIConfig()` (which includes `ThemeProvider`, `PdfViewer`, `ExperimentsTab`) re-renders whenever ANY config refresh occurs (including after saving settings, or when `refreshConfig()` is called from anywhere). The `ThemeProvider` component at `/Users/katisarrow/essay_read/frontend/src/components/ThemeProvider.tsx` re-renders all its children (the entire app layout) on every config change.\n\n**`setHelpCallback` side effect in render** (`/Users/katisarrow/essay_read/frontend/src/components/Layout.tsx`, lines 52-53):\n```tsx\nconst toggleShortcuts = useCallback(() => setShowShortcuts(v => !v), [])\nsetHelpCallback(toggleShortcuts)\n```\n`setHelpCallback` is called directly in the render body (not in a `useEffect`). This is a side effect during render, which is a React anti-pattern. While `useCallback` makes the function reference stable, calling this outside of `useEffect` means it runs on every render of `Layout`, including when `navCollapsed`, `showVersion`, or any other state changes. It should be moved to a `useEffect`.\n\n**`useStreaming` -- Many independent state setters** (`/Users/katisarrow/essay_read/frontend/src/hooks/useStreaming.ts`):\nThis hook has 7 independent `useState` calls (lines 42-47). Each SSE event triggers a `setState` call. Because React batches state updates within the same event handler but SSE events arrive asynchronously, each event triggers a separate re-render. During active analysis streaming, this can mean dozens of re-renders per second. Consider consolidating into a single `useReducer` to batch state updates.\n\n---\n\n### 6. MISSING LAZY LOADING OF HEAVY DEPENDENCIES\n\n**`mermaid`** is imported at module scope in both:\n- `/Users/katisarrow/essay_read/frontend/src/features/analysis/FlowChart.tsx` (line 2)\n- `/Users/katisarrow/essay_read/frontend/src/features/code/CodeAnalysisPanel.tsx` (line 4)\n\n`mermaid` is a large library (~500KB+). Since `FlowChart` is only shown on the Method tab, and `CodeAnalysisPanel` is only shown in the Code tab, these should be dynamically imported.\n\n**`recharts`** is imported at module scope in `/Users/katisarrow/essay_read/frontend/src/features/analysis/tabs/ExperimentsTab.tsx` (lines 3-5):\n```tsx\nimport {\n  BarChart, Bar, LineChart, Line, ScatterChart, Scatter,\n  XAxis, YAxis, Tooltip, Legend, ResponsiveContainer, CartesianGrid,\n} from 'recharts'\n```\nThis is a heavy charting library that is only needed when the user clicks the \"Experiments\" tab. Dynamic import would shrink the initial ReaderPage bundle.\n\n**`react-force-graph-2d`** is imported at module scope in `/Users/katisarrow/essay_read/frontend/src/features/research/GraphCanvas.tsx` (line 2). Since `GraphCanvas` is only shown on the Research page, this could be lazy-loaded at the page level.\n\n---\n\n### 7. LARGE COMPONENTS THAT SHOULD BE SPLIT\n\n**`SettingsPage`** at `/Users/katisarrow/essay_read/frontend/src/pages/SettingsPage.tsx` (1,374 lines) is by far the largest file. It contains `TestButton`, `ConfigSection`, `UIConfigSection`, and the main `SettingsPage` component all in a single file. The `ConfigSection` component (lines 306-507) and `UIConfigSection` (lines 518-694) should be extracted to separate files. Each settings section (translation, analysis, embedding, research, reranker, similarity, arxiv, findpapers) could be its own lazy-loaded component.\n\n**`OverviewTab`** at `/Users/katisarrow/essay_read/frontend/src/features/analysis/tabs/OverviewTab.tsx` (579 lines) contains the import flow with workspace selection, import progress tracking, and results display. The workspace selector modal (lines 516-576) and import flow UI (lines 460-504) should be extracted.\n\n---\n\n### 8. INLINE OBJECT AND FUNCTION CREATION IN JSX\n\n**`WorkspaceDetailPage`** (`/Users/katisarrow/essay_read/frontend/src/pages/WorkspaceDetailPage.tsx`):\nThroughout the file, inline arrow functions in `onClick` handlers create new function references on every render:\n- Line 324: `onNavigate={() => navigate(...)}`\n- Line 325: `onRemove={() => handleRemovePaper(paper.id)}`\n- Line 396-398: similar patterns\n\n**`ResearchPage`** (`/Users/katisarrow/essay_read/frontend/src/pages/ResearchPage.tsx`, lines 137-139):\n```tsx\nonSelectNode={(n) => { setSelectedNode(n); if (n) setSelectedEdge(null) }}\nonSelectEdge={(e) => { setSelectedEdge(e); if (e) setSelectedNode(null) }}\n```\n\n**`Layout`** (`/Users/katisarrow/essay_read/frontend/src/components/Layout.tsx`, lines 251-263):\nInline arrow functions in `onConfirm` and `onCancel` of `ConfirmDialog`.\n\n---\n\n### 9. SPECIFIC OPTIMIZATION RECOMMENDATIONS (Priority Order)\n\n1. **Virtualize PDF pages in PdfViewer** -- This is the highest-impact optimization. Rendering all pages simultaneously is the most resource-intensive operation in the app.\n\n2. **Add React.memo to AnnotationLayer** -- Since each page has its own AnnotationLayer and annotations are shared across all pages, memoizing this component prevents N-1 unnecessary re-renders when one annotation changes.\n\n3. **Memoize AnalysisPanel and its tab children** -- During streaming, partialResult changes cause the entire AnalysisPanel + all 7 tabs to re-render on every field update. Memoizing the tabs would prevent this cascade.\n\n4. **Batch useStreaming state into useReducer** -- Consolidate the 7 state variables into a single reducer to take advantage of React's synchronous batching.\n\n5. **Fix useUIConfig to use referential equality** -- Use `useRef` + shallow comparison or `JSON.stringify` to avoid returning new object references when the config has not actually changed.\n\n6. **Lazy-load mermaid and recharts** -- Use dynamic `import()` inside the components that use them, not at module scope.\n\n7. **Wrap LogLine in React.memo** -- Prevents re-rendering of all 500 log lines when a new log arrives.\n\n8. **Fix the zoom polling interval in GraphCanvas** -- Use a ref for the display value and only trigger state update when the formatted string changes.\n\n9. **Move setHelpCallback to useEffect in Layout** -- Eliminates the render-time side effect.\n\n10. **Lazy-load GraphCanvas behind ResearchPage** -- The `react-force-graph-2d` library is heavy and only used on one page."}]}]},"parent_tool_use_id":null,"session_id":"29257b8f-ede7-46a4-8e0f-a743591d296a","uuid":"e21bf917-35ea-46b9-b67a-c780b98bca1c","timestamp":"2026-06-09T05:11:44.226Z","tool_use_result":{"status":"completed","prompt":"I need to identify performance optimization opportunities in this React + TypeScript frontend. Focus on:\n\n1. Read frontend/src/App.tsx and frontend/src/main.tsx to understand the app structure and routing\n2. Check frontend/src/pages/ for any heavy pages that could benefit from lazy loading\n3. Look at frontend/src/features/analysis/ for any large components that could be memoized or split\n4. Check frontend/src/components/ for frequently re-rendering components\n5. Look at frontend/src/hooks/ for any hooks that might cause unnecessary re-renders\n6. Check if React.memo, useMemo, useCallback are used appropriately\n7. Look for any heavy computations in render paths\n8. Check if code splitting is already implemented (lazy imports)\n\nProvide a detailed report of findings with specific file paths and line numbers.","agentId":"aeb539a7a28be405a","agentType":"Explore","content":[{"type":"text","text":"I now have a thorough understanding of the entire codebase. Here is my detailed performance optimization report.\n\n---\n\n## Performance Optimization Report for essay_read React + TypeScript Frontend\n\n### 1. CODE SPLITTING (Already Implemented -- Well Done)\n\n**File:** `/Users/katisarrow/essay_read/frontend/src/App.tsx`, lines 26-34\n\nAll 9 page routes are already lazy-loaded with `React.lazy()`. The `TerminalPanel` component also lazy-loads the `RealTerminal` module at `/Users/katisarrow/essay_read/frontend/src/components/TerminalPanel.tsx`, line 4. This is a strong baseline -- initial bundle only needs to load the Layout shell and whichever page route the user hits.\n\n**One remaining opportunity:** The heavy feature components inside pages are NOT lazy-loaded. For example, `PdfViewer` (374 lines, imports `react-pdf` + `pdfjs-dist`), `GraphCanvas` (362 lines, imports `react-force-graph-2d`), `FlowChart` (imports `mermaid`), and `CodeAnalysisPanel` (531 lines, imports `mermaid`). These are eagerly bundled within their parent page chunks. Lazy-loading `GraphCanvas`, `FlowChart`, and `CodeAnalysisPanel` behind the tabs that actually render them would further shrink the initial parse cost of the `ReaderPage` and `CodePage` chunks.\n\n---\n\n### 2. ZERO USE OF React.memo\n\n**Finding:** There is not a single `React.memo()` call anywhere in the codebase. The grep for `React.memo` returned zero results.\n\nThis is the single most impactful optimization gap. The following components receive complex, frequently-changing props and would benefit from `React.memo`:\n\n- **`AnnotationLayer`** (`/Users/katisarrow/essay_read/frontend/src/features/viewer/AnnotationLayer.tsx`) -- receives the full `annotations` array (which gets new entries on every create) and a `pageNumber` number. It filters annotations on every render (line 13: `annotations.filter(a => a.page_number === pageNumber)`). With 20+ PDF pages rendered simultaneously in `PdfViewer`, each page re-renders whenever any annotation is added or deleted anywhere.\n\n- **`Sidebar`** (`/Users/katisarrow/essay_read/frontend/src/components/Sidebar.tsx`) -- receives `papers` array that gets fully replaced on fetch. It already has a `useMemo` for filtered papers (line 27), but the component itself re-renders on every parent state change.\n\n- **`PaperList`** (`/Users/katisarrow/essay_read/frontend/src/features/library/PaperList.tsx`) -- 402 lines, receives 17 props. Many are callbacks that change on every render of `LibraryPage` unless wrapped in `useCallback`.\n\n- **`OverviewTab`** (`/Users/katisarrow/essay_read/frontend/src/features/analysis/tabs/OverviewTab.tsx`) -- 579 lines, the largest tab component. It is re-rendered every time the `AnalysisPanel` parent re-renders (e.g., when switching tabs, when the activeTab state changes), even when its props have not changed.\n\n- **`OutlineTab`**, **`MethodTab`**, **`ExperimentsTab`**, **`InterviewTab`** -- all purely display components that receive `result` and should skip re-renders when `result` is referentially stable.\n\n- **`LogLine`** (`/Users/katisarrow/essay_read/frontend/src/components/TerminalPanel.tsx`, line 33) -- rendered in lists of up to 500 entries. Without `React.memo`, adding a new log entry causes ALL existing `LogLine` components to re-render.\n\n- **`ToastContainer`** (`/Users/katisarrow/essay_read/frontend/src/components/ToastContainer.tsx`) -- individual toast items are re-rendered as a batch when any toast is added or dismissed.\n\n---\n\n### 3. UNSTABLE PROP REFERENCES CAUSING UNNECESSARY RE-RENDERS\n\n**`ReaderPage`** (`/Users/katisarrow/essay_read/frontend/src/pages/ReaderPage.tsx`, lines 299-308):\n```tsx\n<PdfViewer\n  ref={pdfViewerRef}\n  pdfUrl={pdfUrl}\n  paperId={paperId}\n  activePage={activePage}\n  onPageChange={onPageChangeNoop}\n/>\n```\nThe `onPageChangeNoop` (line 37) is stable via `useCallback`, which is good. However, `activePage` changes frequently during scroll (via `IntersectionObserver`), and `PdfViewer` is not memoized, so every scroll-triggered page visibility update re-renders the entire PdfViewer.\n\n**`AnalysisPanel`** (`/Users/katisarrow/essay_read/frontend/src/features/analysis/AnalysisPanel.tsx`, lines 343-355):\n```tsx\n<AnalysisPanel\n  result={analysis}\n  partialResult={partialResult}\n  status={status}\n  progressMessage={progressMessage}\n  progress={progress}\n  relatedStatus={relatedStatus}\n  onJumpToPage={handleOutlineJump}\n  paperId={paperId}\n  onStartAnalysis={startAnalysis}\n  onReanalyze={reparse}\n/>\n```\nThe `partialResult` object is created fresh on every SSE `field` event via spread (in `useStreaming.ts` line 115: `setPartialResult(prev => ({ ...prev, [fieldName]: value }))`). This means `AnalysisPanel` re-renders on every field received during streaming analysis, and without memoization, it cascades to all 7 tab components. Since only `LoadingState` needs the partial result during streaming, this is wasteful when the user is on the `done` state viewing an analysis tab.\n\n**`ResearchPage`** (`/Users/katisarrow/essay_read/frontend/src/pages/ResearchPage.tsx`, lines 137-139):\n```tsx\nonSelectNode={(n) => { setSelectedNode(n); if (n) setSelectedEdge(null) }}\nonSelectEdge={(e) => { setSelectedEdge(e); if (e) setSelectedNode(null) }}\n```\nInline arrow functions are created on every render, defeating any future `React.memo` on `GraphCanvas`. These should be wrapped in `useCallback`.\n\n---\n\n### 4. HEAVY COMPUTATIONS IN RENDER PATHS\n\n**`PdfViewer` -- All pages rendered simultaneously** (`/Users/katisarrow/essay_read/frontend/src/features/viewer/PdfViewer.tsx`, lines 263-299):\n```tsx\n{Array.from({ length: numPages }, (_, i) => i + 1).map(pageNum => (\n  <Page pageNumber={pageNum} ... />\n))}\n```\nEvery single page of the PDF is rendered into the DOM simultaneously. For a 30-page paper, this means 30 `react-pdf` `<Page>` components plus 30 `<AnnotationLayer>` components all mount at once. This is extremely expensive in both memory and initial render time. A virtualized approach (rendering only visible pages plus a buffer) would dramatically reduce memory usage and first-paint time.\n\n**`FlowChart` -- Mermaid parsing on every render** (`/Users/katisarrow/essay_read/frontend/src/features/analysis/FlowChart.tsx`, lines 87-115):\nThe `useEffect` at line 87 re-runs `mermaid.render()` whenever the `chart` prop changes. While `mermaid.render()` is async, it is CPU-intensive and generates new DOM. The component does not use `useMemo` to cache the parsed SVG. If the parent re-renders (e.g., because a sibling tab was clicked in `AnalysisPanel`), the chart re-renders even if `chart` has not changed. Wrapping `FlowChart` in `React.memo` would prevent this.\n\n**`GraphCanvas` -- Force simulation polling** (`/Users/katisarrow/essay_read/frontend/src/features/research/GraphCanvas.tsx`, lines 226-233):\n```tsx\nuseEffect(() => {\n  const t = setInterval(() => {\n    const z = fgRef.current?.zoom?.()\n    if (typeof z === 'number' && Math.abs(z - zoomLevel) > 0.01) {\n      setZoomLevel(z)\n    }\n  }, 300)\n  return () => clearInterval(t)\n}, [zoomLevel])\n```\nThis polls every 300ms to sync the zoom level display, calling `setZoomLevel` which triggers a re-render of the entire `GraphCanvas` component (and all its children) even when the zoom percentage displayed in the UI has not meaningfully changed. This is a classic anti-pattern -- use a ref for the displayed value and only update state when the displayed text actually changes.\n\n**`ExperimentsTab` -- CSS variable reads in useMemo** (`/Users/katisarrow/essay_read/frontend/src/features/analysis/tabs/ExperimentsTab.tsx`, lines 23-38):\n`useThemeColors()` reads `getComputedStyle` on every theme change. The dependency array correctly depends on `ui.theme`, but `useUIConfig()` returns a new object reference on every config refresh (line 104 of `useConfig.ts`: `return ui` where `ui` is constructed with spread), which triggers the `useMemo` to recompute even when the theme has not changed.\n\n---\n\n### 5. HOOKS THAT CAUSE UNNECESSARY RE-RENDERS\n\n**`useUIConfig`** (`/Users/katisarrow/essay_read/frontend/src/hooks/useConfig.ts`, lines 85-105):\nEvery call to `useUIConfig()` creates a listener that calls `fetchConfig().then(cfg => setUI({...saved, ...previewOverrides}))`. The `setUI` call always creates a new object reference via spread, even when the config has not changed. This means every component using `useUIConfig()` (which includes `ThemeProvider`, `PdfViewer`, `ExperimentsTab`) re-renders whenever ANY config refresh occurs (including after saving settings, or when `refreshConfig()` is called from anywhere). The `ThemeProvider` component at `/Users/katisarrow/essay_read/frontend/src/components/ThemeProvider.tsx` re-renders all its children (the entire app layout) on every config change.\n\n**`setHelpCallback` side effect in render** (`/Users/katisarrow/essay_read/frontend/src/components/Layout.tsx`, lines 52-53):\n```tsx\nconst toggleShortcuts = useCallback(() => setShowShortcuts(v => !v), [])\nsetHelpCallback(toggleShortcuts)\n```\n`setHelpCallback` is called directly in the render body (not in a `useEffect`). This is a side effect during render, which is a React anti-pattern. While `useCallback` makes the function reference stable, calling this outside of `useEffect` means it runs on every render of `Layout`, including when `navCollapsed`, `showVersion`, or any other state changes. It should be moved to a `useEffect`.\n\n**`useStreaming` -- Many independent state setters** (`/Users/katisarrow/essay_read/frontend/src/hooks/useStreaming.ts`):\nThis hook has 7 independent `useState` calls (lines 42-47). Each SSE event triggers a `setState` call. Because React batches state updates within the same event handler but SSE events arrive asynchronously, each event triggers a separate re-render. During active analysis streaming, this can mean dozens of re-renders per second. Consider consolidating into a single `useReducer` to batch state updates.\n\n---\n\n### 6. MISSING LAZY LOADING OF HEAVY DEPENDENCIES\n\n**`mermaid`** is imported at module scope in both:\n- `/Users/katisarrow/essay_read/frontend/src/features/analysis/FlowChart.tsx` (line 2)\n- `/Users/katisarrow/essay_read/frontend/src/features/code/CodeAnalysisPanel.tsx` (line 4)\n\n`mermaid` is a large library (~500KB+). Since `FlowChart` is only shown on the Method tab, and `CodeAnalysisPanel` is only shown in the Code tab, these should be dynamically imported.\n\n**`recharts`** is imported at module scope in `/Users/katisarrow/essay_read/frontend/src/features/analysis/tabs/ExperimentsTab.tsx` (lines 3-5):\n```tsx\nimport {\n  BarChart, Bar, LineChart, Line, ScatterChart, Scatter,\n  XAxis, YAxis, Tooltip, Legend, ResponsiveContainer, CartesianGrid,\n} from 'recharts'\n```\nThis is a heavy charting library that is only needed when the user clicks the \"Experiments\" tab. Dynamic import would shrink the initial ReaderPage bundle.\n\n**`react-force-graph-2d`** is imported at module scope in `/Users/katisarrow/essay_read/frontend/src/features/research/GraphCanvas.tsx` (line 2). Since `GraphCanvas` is only shown on the Research page, this could be lazy-loaded at the page level.\n\n---\n\n### 7. LARGE COMPONENTS THAT SHOULD BE SPLIT\n\n**`SettingsPage`** at `/Users/katisarrow/essay_read/frontend/src/pages/SettingsPage.tsx` (1,374 lines) is by far the largest file. It contains `TestButton`, `ConfigSection`, `UIConfigSection`, and the main `SettingsPage` component all in a single file. The `ConfigSection` component (lines 306-507) and `UIConfigSection` (lines 518-694) should be extracted to separate files. Each settings section (translation, analysis, embedding, research, reranker, similarity, arxiv, findpapers) could be its own lazy-loaded component.\n\n**`OverviewTab`** at `/Users/katisarrow/essay_read/frontend/src/features/analysis/tabs/OverviewTab.tsx` (579 lines) contains the import flow with workspace selection, import progress tracking, and results display. The workspace selector modal (lines 516-576) and import flow UI (lines 460-504) should be extracted.\n\n---\n\n### 8. INLINE OBJECT AND FUNCTION CREATION IN JSX\n\n**`WorkspaceDetailPage`** (`/Users/katisarrow/essay_read/frontend/src/pages/WorkspaceDetailPage.tsx`):\nThroughout the file, inline arrow functions in `onClick` handlers create new function references on every render:\n- Line 324: `onNavigate={() => navigate(...)}`\n- Line 325: `onRemove={() => handleRemovePaper(paper.id)}`\n- Line 396-398: similar patterns\n\n**`ResearchPage`** (`/Users/katisarrow/essay_read/frontend/src/pages/ResearchPage.tsx`, lines 137-139):\n```tsx\nonSelectNode={(n) => { setSelectedNode(n); if (n) setSelectedEdge(null) }}\nonSelectEdge={(e) => { setSelectedEdge(e); if (e) setSelectedNode(null) }}\n```\n\n**`Layout`** (`/Users/katisarrow/essay_read/frontend/src/components/Layout.tsx`, lines 251-263):\nInline arrow functions in `onConfirm` and `onCancel` of `ConfirmDialog`.\n\n---\n\n### 9. SPECIFIC OPTIMIZATION RECOMMENDATIONS (Priority Order)\n\n1. **Virtualize PDF pages in PdfViewer** -- This is the highest-impact optimization. Rendering all pages simultaneously is the most resource-intensive operation in the app.\n\n2. **Add React.memo to AnnotationLayer** -- Since each page has its own AnnotationLayer and annotations are shared across all pages, memoizing this component prevents N-1 unnecessary re-renders when one annotation changes.\n\n3. **Memoize AnalysisPanel and its tab children** -- During streaming, partialResult changes cause the entire AnalysisPanel + all 7 tabs to re-render on every field update. Memoizing the tabs would prevent this cascade.\n\n4. **Batch useStreaming state into useReducer** -- Consolidate the 7 state variables into a single reducer to take advantage of React's synchronous batching.\n\n5. **Fix useUIConfig to use referential equality** -- Use `useRef` + shallow comparison or `JSON.stringify` to avoid returning new object references when the config has not actually changed.\n\n6. **Lazy-load mermaid and recharts** -- Use dynamic `import()` inside the components that use them, not at module scope.\n\n7. **Wrap LogLine in React.memo** -- Prevents re-rendering of all 500 log lines when a new log arrives.\n\n8. **Fix the zoom polling interval in GraphCanvas** -- Use a ref for the display value and only trigger state update when the formatted string changes.\n\n9. **Move setHelpCallback to useEffect in Layout** -- Eliminates the render-time side effect.\n\n10. **Lazy-load GraphCanvas behind ResearchPage** -- The `react-force-graph-2d` library is heavy and only used on one page."}],"totalDurationMs":96604,"totalTokens":157920,"totalToolUseCount":53,"usage":{"input_tokens":12089,"cache_creation_input_tokens":0,"cache_read_input_tokens":142208,"output_tokens":3623,"server_tool_use":{"web_search_requests":0,"web_fetch_requests":0},"service_tier":"standard","cache_creation":{"ephemeral_1h_input_tokens":0,"ephemeral_5m_input_tokens":0},"inference_geo":"","iterations":[],"speed":"standard"},"toolStats":{"readCount":44,"searchCount":2,"bashCount":7,"editFileCount":0,"linesAdded":0,"linesRemoved":0,"otherToolCount":0}}}
